{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if any two elements in a given list of numbers are closer to each other\n    than a specified threshold.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold: The non-negative distance threshold.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if a pair of elements with a difference less than the threshold exists,\n        False otherwise.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If 'numbers' is not a list or contains non-numeric types,\n                   or if 'threshold' is not a numeric type.\n        ValueError: If 'threshold' is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 3.1. Input Validation Component\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Threshold must be a numeric type.\")\n    if threshold < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Threshold cannot be negative.\")\n\n    if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'numbers' must be a list.\")\n\n    if not all(isinstance(n, (int, float)) for n in numbers):\n        raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be numeric.\")\n\n    # 3.2. Core Logic Component\n    \n    # Edge Case Handling: Fewer than two elements means no pairs exist.\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Sorting: Create a sorted copy to avoid modifying the original list.\n    # This is the O(N log N) step.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # Linear Scan & Comparison: Iterate through adjacent elements.\n    # This is the O(N) step.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Calculate the difference between the current and next element.\n        difference = sorted_numbers[i + 1] - sorted_numbers[i]\n\n        # Early Exit: If a close pair is found, return True immediately.\n        if difference < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    # Default Return: If the loop completes, no close pair was found.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if any two numbers in a list are closer than a given threshold.\n\n    This function provides an efficient O(n log n) solution by first sorting\n    the list and then comparing only adjacent elements.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers to be analyzed.\n        threshold: The proximity threshold. The function checks if the absolute\n                   difference between any two numbers is less than this value.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if a pair of numbers closer than the threshold is found,\n        otherwise False.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Handle Edge Cases: A list with fewer than two elements cannot have a pair.\n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # 2. Sort the List: Create a sorted copy to avoid modifying the original list.\n    # This is the O(n log n) step.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # 3. Iterate and Compare Adjacent Elements: Loop up to the second-to-last element.\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        # 4. Calculate Difference: For a sorted list, the smallest differences\n        # will always be between adjacent elements.\n        # Since sorted_numbers[i+1] >= sorted_numbers[i], abs() is not needed.\n        diff = sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i]\n\n        # 5. Check Against Threshold: If we find a pair closer than the threshold,\n        # we can return True immediately.\n        if diff < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    # 6. Return False: If the loop completes, no such pair was found.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if any two numbers within a given list are closer to each other\n    than a specified threshold.\n\n    This function implements an efficient O(N log N) algorithm by first sorting\n    the list and then performing a single linear scan to check for close\n    adjacent elements.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers to be checked.\n        threshold: A non-negative float representing the distance threshold.\n                   Any pair of numbers with a difference less than this value\n                   is considered \"close\".\n\n    Returns:\n        True if any two numbers in the list have an absolute difference\n        less than the threshold. False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 3.2.1. Step 1: Input Validation & Edge Case Handling\n    # Check if the list has fewer than two elements.\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # A negative threshold is illogical for a distance measurement.\n    if threshold < 0:\n        return False\n\n    # 3.2.1. Step 2: Sorting (Preprocessing Layer)\n    # Create a sorted copy of the input list.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # 3.2.1. Step 3: Linear Scan (Comparison Layer)\n    # Iterate through the sorted list up to the second-to-last element.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Calculate the difference between the current and the next element.\n        # No absolute value is needed as sorted_numbers[i+1] >= sorted_numbers[i].\n        diff = sorted_numbers[i + 1] - sorted_numbers[i]\n\n        # If the difference is less than the threshold, a close pair is found.\n        if diff < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    # 3.2.1. Step 4: Final Result\n    # If the loop completes, no adjacent pair was closer than the threshold.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a list of numbers contains any pair of elements whose\n    absolute difference is less than a specified threshold.\n\n    This function implements an O(n log n) algorithm by first sorting the list\n    and then performing a single pass to compare adjacent elements.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: The list of numbers to be checked.\n        threshold: The non-negative distance threshold.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if any two elements in the list have a difference less than\n        the threshold, otherwise False.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the provided threshold is a negative number.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Input Validation\n    if threshold < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Threshold cannot be negative\")\n\n    # 2. Handle edge cases for list size\n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # 3. Create a sorted copy to avoid modifying the original list (no side effects)\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # 4. Iterate and compare adjacent elements\n    # We only need to check adjacent elements because if two numbers a and c\n    # have a difference less than the threshold, any number b between them in\n    # the sorted list will have an even smaller difference with a or c.\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        # Since the list is sorted, sorted_numbers[i+1] >= sorted_numbers[i]\n        difference = sorted_numbers[i + 1] - sorted_numbers[i]\n        if difference < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    # 5. If no close elements are found after checking all adjacent pairs\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Efficiently checks if any two numbers in a list are closer than a given threshold.\n\n    This function implements an O(n log n) algorithm by first sorting the list\n    and then comparing only adjacent elements.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold: A non-negative floating-point number representing the\n                   proximity limit.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if a pair of numbers with an absolute difference less than\n        the threshold is found; otherwise, False.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Edge Case Handling\n    # If the list has fewer than two elements, no pairs can be formed.\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Step 2: Sorting\n    # Create a sorted copy to avoid mutating the original list.\n    # The complexity of this step is O(n log n).\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # Step 3 & 4: Adjacent Pair Iteration and Proximity Check\n    # Iterate through the sorted list, comparing each element to its neighbor.\n    # This loop runs n-1 times, making it O(n).\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Since the list is sorted, the difference will be non-negative.\n        diff = sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i]\n        \n        # If the difference is less than the threshold, we found a close pair.\n        if diff < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    # Step 5: Default Return\n    # If the loop completes, no pair was found that met the condition.\n    return False\n```"}
