{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing groups of parentheses, ignores whitespace, and\n    separates the top-level, balanced groups into a list of individual strings.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string expected to contain parentheses and whitespace.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of strings, where each string is a well-formed, balanced,\n        top-level parenthesis group from the input.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Initialization\n    result_groups: List[str] = []\n    balance_counter = 0\n    # Using a list of characters is more efficient for building strings in a loop\n    current_group_builder: List[str] = []\n\n    # 2. Input Pre-processing: Remove all whitespace\n    cleaned_string = \"\".join(paren_string.split())\n\n    # 3. Iteration and Processing\n    for char in cleaned_string:\n        if char == '(':\n            # If we are already inside a top-level group, this char is part of its content\n            if balance_counter > 0:\n                current_group_builder.append(char)\n            # Every '(' increases the nesting level\n            balance_counter += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            # Only process ')' if it has a matching '('\n            if balance_counter > 0:\n                balance_counter -= 1\n                \n                # If the balance is now 0, we have closed a top-level group\n                if balance_counter == 0:\n                    # The builder holds the inner content.\n                    # We form the complete group by adding the outer parentheses.\n                    content = \"\".join(current_group_builder)\n                    result_groups.append(f\"({content})\")\n                    # Reset the builder for the next potential group\n                    current_group_builder = []\n                else:\n                    # If balance is not 0, we are still inside a group, so append the ')'\n                    current_group_builder.append(char)\n\n    # 4. Finalization: Return the collected groups\n    return result_groups\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing parentheses and spaces, and extracts all \n    top-level, balanced parenthetical groups into a list of strings.\n    \n    Args:\n        paren_string: The input string containing zero or more groups of \n                      nested parentheses. The string is expected to contain \n                      only '(', ')', and space characters.\n                      \n    Returns:\n        A list where each element is a string representing a top-level, \n        balanced group of parentheses found in the input string, in the \n        order of their appearance. The returned strings will not contain any spaces.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 2. Preprocessing: Create clean_string by removing all spaces.\n    clean_string = paren_string.replace(' ', '')\n\n    # 1. Initialization:\n    groups: List[str] = []\n    balance_counter: int = 0\n    group_start_index: int = 0\n\n    # 3. Iteration: Loop through each character and its index.\n    for i, char in enumerate(clean_string):\n        # 4. Opening Parenthesis:\n        if char == '(':\n            # a. If at base level, mark the start of a new top-level group.\n            if balance_counter == 0:\n                group_start_index = i\n            # b. Increment the nesting depth.\n            balance_counter += 1\n        # 5. Closing Parenthesis:\n        elif char == ')':\n            # a. Decrement the nesting depth.\n            balance_counter -= 1\n            # b. If returning to base level, a top-level group has ended.\n            if balance_counter == 0:\n                group = clean_string[group_start_index : i + 1]\n                groups.append(group)\n    \n    # 6. Completion: Return the list of identified groups.\n    return groups\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing multiple, non-overlapping groups of balanced\n    parentheses and separates them into a list of individual group strings.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: The string containing parentheses groups, expected to be\n                      a valid, balanced string with only parentheses and spaces.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list where each element is a string representing a single,\n        top-level, balanced parenthesis group.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize an empty list to store the results.\n    groups: List[str] = []\n    \n    # Initialize a list to build the current group (more efficient than string concat).\n    current_group_builder = []\n    \n    # Initialize an integer to track the nesting level.\n    nesting_level = 0\n\n    # Pre-process the input string by removing all space characters.\n    sanitized_string = paren_string.replace(\" \", \"\")\n\n    # Iterate through each character in the processed string.\n    for char in sanitized_string:\n        if char == '(':\n            # Increment nesting_level for an opening parenthesis.\n            nesting_level += 1\n        \n        # Append the character to the current group being built.\n        current_group_builder.append(char)\n\n        if char == ')':\n            # Decrement nesting_level for a closing parenthesis.\n            nesting_level -= 1\n        \n        # Boundary Check: If nesting_level is 0, a top-level group is complete.\n        # The builder must not be empty, which is implicitly true if nesting_level\n        # just returned to 0 from a positive value.\n        if nesting_level == 0 and current_group_builder:\n            # A top-level group has been completed.\n            # Append the content of the builder to the groups list.\n            groups.append(\"\".join(current_group_builder))\n            \n            # Reset the builder to begin capturing the next group.\n            current_group_builder = []\n\n    # After the loop completes, return the list of captured groups.\n    return groups\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing multiple, non-overlapping groups of nested\n    parentheses and separates them into a list of individual group strings.\n\n    Based on the System Design Document: Parenthesis Group Separator.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: The input string containing only '(', ')', and space\n                      characters.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of strings, where each string is a distinct, top-level,\n        balanced parenthesis group.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_groups: List[str] = []\n    balance_counter = 0\n    current_group_builder = []\n\n    for char in paren_string:\n        if char.isspace():\n            continue\n\n        # Append the non-space character to the current group being built\n        current_group_builder.append(char)\n\n        if char == '(':\n            balance_counter += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            balance_counter -= 1\n\n        # A balance_counter of 0 signifies the end of a top-level group\n        # We also check if the builder is non-empty to avoid processing\n        # potential leading spaces.\n        if balance_counter == 0 and current_group_builder:\n            result_groups.append(\"\".join(current_group_builder))\n            current_group_builder = []\n\n    return result_groups\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string of parentheses and separates it into a list of top-level, \n    balanced parenthesis groups.\n\n    This function implements a counter-based, single-pass algorithm to identify\n    and extract all distinct, top-level, balanced groups of parentheses from the\n    input string. Whitespace is ignored.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses,\n                      potentially with spaces.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list where each element is a string representing a single, top-level,\n        balanced group of parentheses found in the input. The order of groups \n        is preserved.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input string contains unbalanced or improperly \n                    nested parentheses, such as a closing parenthesis before an\n                    opening one (e.g., '())(') or unclosed parentheses at the\n                    end (e.g., '(()').\n    \"\"\"\n    # 2. Preprocessing: Create a clean version of the input string by removing all whitespace.\n    clean_string = \"\".join(paren_string.split())\n\n    if not clean_string:\n        return []\n\n    # 1. Initialization\n    result_groups: List[str] = []\n    balance_counter: int = 0\n    group_start_index: int = 0\n\n    # 3. Iteration\n    for i, char in enumerate(clean_string):\n        # 4. Balance Tracking\n        if char == '(':\n            balance_counter += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            balance_counter -= 1\n        else:\n            # Ignore other characters as they don't affect balance.\n            pass\n\n        # 5. Error Detection (Premature Close)\n        if balance_counter < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid parentheses: premature closing parenthesis found.\")\n\n        # 6. Group Completion\n        if balance_counter == 0:\n            # a. Extract the substring\n            group = clean_string[group_start_index : i + 1]\n            # b. Append this substring to the result_groups list\n            result_groups.append(group)\n            # c. Update group_start_index for the next potential group\n            group_start_index = i + 1\n\n    # 7. Final Validation\n    if balance_counter != 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid parentheses: unclosed opening parentheses remain.\")\n\n    # 8. Return\n    return result_groups\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing groups of parentheses and separates it into a list\n    of distinct, top-level, balanced parenthetical groups.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: The input string containing parentheses and spaces.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of strings, where each string is a top-level parenthetical group\n        with whitespace removed.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize a results list to store the identified groups.\n    groups: List[str] = []\n\n    # Pre-process the input string by removing all whitespace characters.\n    # The split() and join() methods handle various whitespace types (spaces, tabs, etc.).\n    clean_string = \"\".join(paren_string.split())\n\n    # If the string is empty after cleaning, there are no groups to find.\n    if not clean_string:\n        return []\n\n    # Initialize a balance counter to track the nesting level of parentheses.\n    balance_counter = 0\n    # Initialize a marker for the start index of the current potential group.\n    group_start_index = 0\n\n    # Iterate through the cleaned string with index and character.\n    for i, char in enumerate(clean_string):\n        if char == '(':\n            # Increment balance for an opening parenthesis.\n            balance_counter += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            # Decrement balance for a closing parenthesis.\n            balance_counter -= 1\n\n        # Check for the completion of a top-level group.\n        # A group is considered top-level and complete when the balance counter\n        # returns to zero.\n        if balance_counter == 0:\n            # Extract the substring for the completed group.\n            # Slicing is from group_start_index up to and including the current index i.\n            group = clean_string[group_start_index : i + 1]\n\n            # Append the extracted group to the results list.\n            groups.append(group)\n\n            # Update the start index for the next potential group.\n            group_start_index = i + 1\n\n    # Return the final list of separated groups.\n    return groups\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and extracts distinct, top-level balanced parenthesis groups\n    from an input string.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string containing groups of nested parentheses.\n                      Whitespace characters are ignored.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of strings, where each string is a validated top-level group,\n        in the order of their appearance.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1.d. Create processed_string by removing all whitespace characters.\n    processed_string = \"\".join(paren_string.split())\n\n    # 1.a. Create an empty list result_groups.\n    result_groups: List[str] = []\n    # 1.b. Initialize balance_counter = 0.\n    balance_counter = 0\n    # 1.c. Initialize group_start_index = 0.\n    group_start_index = 0\n\n    # 2.a. Loop through processed_string with index i and character char.\n    for i, char in enumerate(processed_string):\n        # 2.b. If char is '(':\n        if char == '(':\n            # 2.b.i. If balance_counter is 0, start of a new top-level group.\n            if balance_counter == 0:\n                group_start_index = i\n            # 2.b.ii. Increment balance_counter.\n            balance_counter += 1\n        # 2.c. If char is ')':\n        elif char == ')':\n            # This check prevents errors on malformed strings like \")(\"\n            if balance_counter > 0:\n                # 2.c.i. Decrement balance_counter.\n                balance_counter -= 1\n                # 2.c.ii. If balance_counter is now 0, end of a top-level group.\n                if balance_counter == 0:\n                    group = processed_string[group_start_index : i + 1]\n                    result_groups.append(group)\n\n    # 3.a. After the loop completes, return result_groups.\n    return result_groups\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Processes a string containing multiple, potentially nested groups of\n    parentheses and separates the top-level, balanced groups into a list of\n    individual strings.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: The input string containing spaces and groups of\n                      parentheses. Assumed to contain balanced top-level groups.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list where each element is a string representing a distinct, top-level,\n        balanced group of parentheses found in the input string, in the order\n        of their appearance.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_groups: List[str] = []\n    balance_counter: int = 0\n    group_start_index: int = 0\n    \n    cleaned_string = \"\".join(paren_string.split())\n\n    for i, char in enumerate(cleaned_string):\n        if char == '(':\n            if balance_counter == 0:\n                group_start_index = i\n            balance_counter += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            balance_counter -= 1\n            if balance_counter == 0:\n                group = cleaned_string[group_start_index : i + 1]\n                result_groups.append(group)\n    \n    return result_groups\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses. Your goal is to\n    separate those group into separate strings and return the list of those.\n    Separate groups are balanced (each open brace is properly closed) and not nested within each other\n    Ignore any spaces in the input string.\n    >>> separate_paren_groups('( ) (( )) (( )( ))')\n    ['()', '(())', '(()())']\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1.d: Pre-process the input paren_string by removing all space characters.\n    processed_string = paren_string.replace(' ', '')\n\n    # 1.a, 1.b, 1.c: Initialization\n    result_groups: List[str] = []\n    balance_counter: int = 0\n    current_group: List[str] = []\n\n    # 2.a: Loop through each character of the processed string.\n    for char in processed_string:\n        # 2.b: Append char to current_group.\n        current_group.append(char)\n\n        # 2.c: If char is '(', increment balance_counter.\n        if char == '(':\n            balance_counter += 1\n        # 2.d: If char is ')', decrement balance_counter.\n        elif char == ')':\n            balance_counter -= 1\n\n        # 3.a: Check if balance_counter has returned to 0.\n        # 3.b: If balance_counter is 0 and current_group is not empty, a group is complete.\n        if balance_counter == 0 and current_group:\n            # 3.b.ii: Join the characters to form a string.\n            # 3.b.iii: Append this new string to the result_groups list.\n            result_groups.append(\"\".join(current_group))\n            # 3.b.iv: Reset current_group to prepare for the next potential group.\n            current_group = []\n\n    # 4.a: After the loop completes, return the result_groups list.\n    return result_groups\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/2", "solution": "```python\nimport numbers\n\ndef truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Extracts the decimal part of a positive floating-point number.\n\n    Args:\n        number: A positive floating-point number.\n\n    Returns:\n        The decimal part of the number, in the range [0.0, 1.0).\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input is not a number.\n        ValueError: If the input number is not positive.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Input Validation\n    if not isinstance(number, numbers.Number):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a number.\")\n\n    if number <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input number must be positive.\")\n\n    # 2. Calculation & 3. Return\n    # Note: floating point precision issues can exist, e.g., 2.1 % 1 might be\n    # 0.10000000000000009. This is expected behavior for standard floats.\n    return number % 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/2", "solution": "```python\ndef truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Isolates and returns the decimal (fractional) part of a positive float.\n\n    This function calculates the decimal part of a given positive floating-point\n    number using the modulo operator, which is efficient and idiomatic.\n\n    Args:\n        number (float): The positive floating-point number from which to\n                        extract the decimal part.\n\n    Returns:\n        float: The decimal part of the input number. This value will always be\n               in the range [0.0, 1.0).\n    \"\"\"\n    # The remainder of a positive number divided by 1 is its decimal part.\n    return number % 1\n\n```"}
