{"task_id": "HumanEval/2", "solution": "```python\ndef truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive floating point number, it decomposes the number and returns its decimal part.\n\n    Args:\n        number (float): The positive floating-point number from which to extract the decimal part.\n\n    Returns:\n        float: The decimal part of the input number. This will always be a value in the range [0.0, 1.0).\n    \n    Example:\n        >>> truncate_number(3.5)\n        0.5\n    \"\"\"\n    # The modulo operator with 1 gives the remainder after division by 1,\n    # which is equivalent to the decimal part for positive numbers.\n    return number % 1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/2", "solution": "```python\ndef truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Isolates the decimal (or fractional) part of a given positive floating-point number.\n\n    This function takes a positive float and returns its decimal part, which will\n    always be in the range [0.0, 1.0).\n\n    Args:\n        number: A positive floating-point number (int is also acceptable).\n\n    Returns:\n        The decimal part of the input number as a float.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input is not a numeric type (int or float).\n        ValueError: If the input number is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(number, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a numeric type (e.g., int, float).\")\n\n    if number < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input number cannot be negative.\")\n\n    # The modulo operator with 1.0 is an efficient and direct way to get the\n    # fractional remainder of a positive number.\n    # e.g., 5.75 % 1.0 -> 0.75\n    # e.g., 10.0 % 1.0 -> 0.0\n    return number % 1.0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/2", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\" Given a positive floating point number, it can be decomposed into\n    and integer part (largest integer smaller than given number) and decimals\n    (leftover part always smaller than 1).\n\n    Return the decimal part of the number.\n    >>> truncate_number(3.5)\n    0.5\n    \"\"\"\n    return number - math.floor(number)\n```"}
